SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 307 | Monday, March 9, 2026

Good morning.

1) NRF port tracker: March imports now seen at 2.05M TEU
That is a revision up from 1.94M, and the first upward revision since last summer. The trade group credits "modest restocking" and earlier-than-usual back-to-school ordering. April is still forecast down year on year because of last April's tariff-driven front-loading.

2) Canadian rail: the two sides are still talking, which is the news
The union's strike deadline is Wednesday 00:01 Eastern. Both Class I railroads have started embargoing hazmat and refrigerated shipments as of yesterday, which is standard 72 hours ahead of a possible stoppage. US shippers of Canadian grain, potash and lumber should assume a week of disruption even if a deal lands.

3) Frostline acquires two cold-chain telemetry startups
The reefer-monitoring company bought both a sensor maker and a small analytics shop for a combined $85M. Cold-chain telemetry has had eleven venture-backed companies chasing perhaps four real buyers; consolidation was overdue. Customers on the acquired platforms have been told nothing changes "for now."

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 7.1%. Warehouse vacancy in the top 20 US markets, the highest since 2013. Landlords are offering six months free on a five-year term in the Inland Empire.

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

